    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="how-to-play" aria-label="How to play Huepaw">
      <h2>How to Play the Colour Gradient Puzzle</h2>
      <p>Huepaw takes a perfectly smooth colour ramp — hue running left to right, brightness running top to bottom — and shuffles every tile out of place. Your job is to put the gradient back together. It is the same idea behind <strong>Huedle</strong> and other daily colour-sorting games, built here as a browser puzzle you can pick up anywhere.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pick up a tile</strong> — tap or click it once. It lifts and glows so you know it is selected.</li>
        <li><strong>Swap it</strong> — tap any other tile. The two exchange places. Tapping the same tile again cancels the selection.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch the flow</strong> — correct neighbours blend into each other. A seam between two tiles means at least one of them is in the wrong place.</li>
        <li><strong>Finish the board</strong> — when every tile sits in its home spot the gradient is seamless and the board clears.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Each board is generated from its date, so the daily puzzle is identical for every player and changes at midnight. Practice mode gives you an unlimited stream of random boards in 3×3, 4×4 or 5×5.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="tips" aria-label="Tips and strategy">
      <h2>Tips for Solving a Gradient Puzzle Faster</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Corner first.</strong> The four corners are the most extreme colours — lightest, deepest, and the two hue endpoints. Placing them halves the search space immediately.</li>
        <li><strong>Work one row at a time.</strong> Brightness is constant across a row in this variant, so all tiles in the top row belong to the same lightness band. Sort the row, then move down.</li>
        <li><strong>Follow the seam.</strong> Two tiles that blend perfectly are probably neighbours in the final layout. Build small correct clusters instead of hunting single tiles.</li>
        <li><strong>Use hints on the last five tiles.</strong> A hint shows two tiles that belong swapped — late in the game that saves more time than it does at the start.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn labels on in low light.</strong> Numbers give every tile a stable identity, which also helps if you have colour-vision deficiency.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="huedle" aria-label="Huepaw vs Huedle">
      <h2>Is This the Same as Huedle?</h2>
      <p><strong>Huedle</strong> (huedle.uk) is an independent daily colour-gradient game — a small, ad-free personal project that swaps tiles until a gradient is restored, with a new board each day. Huepaw is GridPaw's own take on that genre rather than a copy of it: same core idea, our own generator, our own boards, and extra modes (three grid sizes, hints, target peek, colour-blind labels, practice mode).</p>
      <p>If you came here looking for <em>huedle hints</em>, <em>huedle answer today</em> or a <em>huedle alternative</em>, the short answer is: Huepaw will not give you the daily solution for another site's board — it gives you a fresh gradient puzzle of your own, plus the technique that makes any gradient board click: build from the corners inward, keep brightness bands together, and trust the seams.</p>
    </section>

